==Solution 2 - Common Sense==

With a quick mental calculation, 60 * 30 yields 1800, which is roughly where 4 of our 5 answer choices lie in. However, we can tell that each square would require at most 2 toothpicks that uniquely belong to itself, so the answer would be <imath>60\cdot 30 \cdot 2</imath> which would be roughly <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(E)}\ 3932}</imath>.

-pearlseal34, Minor Edit by MagentaButterfly29

==Solution 3 - Deriving a Formula==
If you wanted to find the number of toothpicks in a x by y rectangle you will notice a pattern. We can separate the toothpicks into 2 groups, vertical and horizontal toothpicks and add them together. There is x(y+1) vertical toothpick that are vertical and y(x+1) horizontal toothpicks.If my explantation doesn't make sense try drawing it out. You can add these two for the total amount of toothpicks. Now we can plug in 60 into x and 32 into y and solve. 60(32+1)+32(60+1)= 60(32)+60+32(60)+32=3932 which is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(E)}\ 3932}</imath>.

-ZenJacob

==Solution 4 - Border vs. Internal toothpicks==
The total area of the 60 X 32 grid is 1920 square units. If each square was separated, we would need 1920 * 4 = 7680 toothpicks. However, once all the squares are joined to form a grid, each internal toothpick forms the edge of two adjacent squares. The perimeter/border of the grid contains 60 + 60 + 32 + 32 = 184 sticks. So, 7680 - 184 = 7496 toothpicks are internal toothpicks. Since each internal toothpick forms the edge of two adjacent squares, we need only 7496 / 2 = 3748 of them. So, the total toothpicks needed are 3748 (internal) + 184 (border) which is <imath>\boxed{\textbf{(E)}\ 3932}</imath>.

-AbbasK

